<p> While true dinosaurs mostly lived on land, the massive Spinosaurus adapted to a water-dwelling lifestyle by using a paddle-like tail to swim through ancient rivers and hunt large fish. Meanwhile, the oceans of the dinosaur world were dominated by terrifying marine reptiles like the long-necked plesiosaurs and the giant, lizard-like mosasaurs. These ocean predators developed flippers instead of legs, allowing them to cruise through the deep water much like modern whales and sharks. Though people often call these ancient sea monsters "dinosaurs," they actually belonged to a completely separate branch of the reptile family tree.</p>
