        </ol>
        
        Millions of years before them, giant plant-eaters like <strong><em>Diplodocus</em></strong>, with its super long neck, and <strong><em>Stegosaurus</em></strong>, with the pointy plates on its back, ruled the land. Because the ground in the American West was just right for saving old bones, scientists have found thousands of amazing fossils there. <p>Over in Asia, especially in China and Mongolia, scientists have found very different kinds of dinosaurs. The dry Gobi Desert is where the quick, smart <strong><em>Velociraptor</em></strong> lived. China is extra famous because its rocks have saved more than just bones—they also saved footprints, skin, and even feathers! This is how we found <strong><em>Sinosauropteryx</em></strong>, a small dinosaur covered in fuzzy feathers. Finding this little creature proved to the world that ancient dinosaurs are actually the grandparents of the birds we see outside today. </p> If you travel down to South America and Africa, you find the real giants of the dinosaur world. In Argentina, scientists dug up <strong><em>Argentinosaurus</em></strong>, a plant-eater that was longer than three school buses parked end-to-end! They also found <strong><em>Spinosaurus</em></strong> in Africa, a huge meat-eater with a sail on its back that spent its time swimming and catching fish like a giant crocodile. Even cold Antarctica had its own dinosaurs, showing that these amazing creatures found a way to live in every single part of the world.</p>
